---
title: Portable bindings
summary: The marked import binding IS the island — a virtual wrapper you can pass around like any component.
related:
  - islands/client-islands
  - reference/constraints
---

When you mark an import, ogygia rewrites the **binding itself** into a virtual island wrapper. The template keeps `<A />`, but `A` is now a portable component you can use anywhere a Svelte component goes.

> **Why this matters.** Because the marked import is a normal component value, islands compose the way Svelte components already do — assign them, pass them around, render them in a list. There is no special island slot or registry to learn. This page is mostly *why* the model is ergonomic; you rarely think about it directly.

```svelte
<script>
  import A from './A.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
</script>

<A start={3} />

<!-- dynamic -->
{@const Active = A}
<Active start={3} />

<!-- in a list -->
{#each [{ Comp: A, props }] as { Comp, props }}
  <Comp {...props} />
{/each}
```

No `<svelte:component>` needed — this is Svelte 5.

## Dedupe by identity

Wrappers are deduped by **component path + strategy/options**, not by tag site or host. The same marked import used a thousand times across a page produces **one** wrapper module and **one** client entry chunk. Every instance shares that entry URL, and each still gets its own region and props payload at SSR (a deferred island's signature stays per-instance).

So `<A />` × 1000 ships one module, not one thousand.

Here is one marked import rendered three times on this page. All three share a single client entry chunk, yet each hydrates into its own independent state:

<script>
  import LoadLive from './LoadLive.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
</script>

<div class="doc-demo-row">
  <div class="doc-demo"><LoadLive /></div>
  <div class="doc-demo"><LoadLive /></div>
  <div class="doc-demo"><LoadLive /></div>
</div>

<style>
  .doc-demo-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(11rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }
  .doc-demo {
    padding: 1.25rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-md, 12px);
    background: var(--bg-sunken);
  }
</style>

## Props, not children

Props are real Svelte props into the wrapper, serialized with devalue. What you **cannot** pass across the boundary is host children or snippets — put UI and lakes inside the island's own `.svelte` file. The one exception is the reserved `ogygiaFallback` snippet on a [server island](/docs/islands/server-islands).

See [Constraints](/docs/reference/constraints) for the full list of what crosses the boundary and what does not.


